// Chapter 3, "Check the Year." Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-14):
// ISACA Certification Exam Candidate Guide, version 1.26 (2026), Appendix A, CISA Examination Content Outline (effective August
//   2024): topics 2A2 organizational structure, IT governance, and IT strategy; 2A3 IT policies, standards, procedures, and
//   practices; 2A5 enterprise risk management; 2B3 IT performance monitoring and reporting; 1B5 reporting and communication
//   techniques; 5A1 information asset security policies, frameworks, standards, and guidelines (taught in the lesson, quiz only).
//   Quiz items 4 (1A4) and 8 (1B3) are spaced review of chapter 1; COBIT 2019 performance management (capability levels 0 to 5):
//   https://www.isaca.org/-/media/files/isacadp/project/isaca/certification/exam-candidate-guides/2026/exam-candidate-guide.pdf
//   https://www.isaca.org/credentialing/cisa/cisa-exam-content-outline
// ITAF, 4th Edition, standards text used for the substance of the reporting lesson (report contents: objectives, scope, period,
//   limitations, findings, conclusions, recommendations, management responses; discussing findings with management before
//   finalizing; follow up), named by topic, never by number, per the course bible:
//   https://www.famu.edu/administration/audit/pdf/standards/ITAF-4th-Edition.pdf
// COBIT (ISACA's governance and management framework; COBIT 2019 separates the governance objective Evaluate, Direct and Monitor
//   from the management objectives, which end in Monitor, Evaluate and Assess): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COBIT
// Balanced scorecard: Kaplan and Norton, Harvard Business Review, 1992; four perspectives, financial, customer, internal process,
//   learning and growth: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balanced_scorecard
// Annualized loss expectancy: ALE = ARO x SLE: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annualized_loss_expectancy
// Colorado State Capitol mile high steps on the west entrance: 1909, "One Mile Above Sea Level" carved into the 15th step; 1969,
//   Colorado State University students' survey, marker on the 18th step; 2003, more precise survey, marker on the 13th step:
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colorado_State_Capitol and https://thecoloradoflyover.com/trivia/saturday-september-26-2026/
//   2026, a NOAA National Geodetic Survey resurvey with improved height models put the mark on the 17th step, the fourth move
//   (Denverite and CPR, July 14, 2026, headline and summary only; Denver7, "Mile high marker moves again"):
//   https://www.denver7.com/news/colorado-150/history-repeats-itself-mile-high-mark-at-the-colorado-state-capitol-moves-for-a-fourth-time
//   The chapter calls the 2026 mark "the newest mark of all, from last summer's survey" and doesn't call it brass, permanent,
//   or temporary: a permanent marker may wait for the new national datum (not verified).
// Dates: Friday, March 12, 2027 (March 9 was a Tuesday). Denver is UTC minus 7 until US daylight saving starts Sunday, March 14,
//   2027, so seven hours behind London: 11:05 AM in Denver is 6:05 PM in London, 3:40 PM is 10:40 PM. Monday is March 15.
// Figures (drill, re-derived): inherent 4 x 5 = 20; residual 1 x 5 = 5, under the board's line of 8; 2019 ALE $5,000,000 x 0.02 =
//   $100,000; the $41,000,000 claimed as fraud / $100,000 = 410 years of expected loss. Policy approved March 2019 with annual
//   review: reviews due March 2020 to March 2026 = 7, none done. Quiz ALE: $200,000 x (1 / 4) = $50,000.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cisa=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa.full||[]).push({
  num: 3, part: `Part One: The Folder`, title: `Check the Year`, weeks: `Friday, March 12`,
  tasks: [`2A2`,`2A3`,`2A5`,`2B3`,`1B5`,`5A1`],
  cast: [
    [`Carla Wynn`,`Meridian's chief financial officer. Everything at Meridian that checks anything reports to her.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `The west steps of the State Capitol face the mountains, which is lovely at sunset and no use at all at twenty past six on a Friday morning in March, when the sun is still somewhere behind the building and the granite has held on to the whole night's cold. Henry Ashby stood at the bottom with his hands in his coat pockets.`,
    `He'd walked past these steps for eleven months. This morning he went up them, one at a time, counting out loud like a man checking his change. On the thirteenth he found a small brass marker. On the fifteenth, cut into the face of the step in letters worn soft at the edges, were the words ONE MILE ABOVE SEA LEVEL. The seventeenth carried the newest mark of all, from last summer's survey. The eighteenth had another brass marker, older and duller. Four steps, each of them a mile high, and no two of them the same step.`,
    `Lou Mancuso came up from Colfax at half past with two coffees and a manila envelope under his arm. He handed Henry a coffee, then the envelope. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Copies. Like I said. Don't make me regret the 'like I said.'"</span>`,
    `Inside was a photocopy of the folded tour map from Dot's handbag, flattened out, with her green circle around one paragraph. Inside the circle she'd underlined the four years, each one twice.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="State Capitol tour map, photocopy, Dot's green circle">The Mile High Steps. In 1909 the words "One Mile Above Sea Level" were carved into the 15th step. In 1969 a survey by Colorado State University students put the mile high point on the 18th step. In 2003 a more precise survey moved it to the 13th. In 2026 a new federal survey moved it again, to the 17th.</prop>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"So which one's right?"</span> Lou said.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"All of them. In their year."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"That's a lawyer's answer."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"The step never moved. What changed was what they measured it against."</span> Henry looked up at the dome, which was just beginning to catch the light. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Every marker was true the day somebody set it. Nobody went back and took the old ones out."</span>`,
    `Lou drank some coffee and thought about it. Then he said, as if it were the same subject, <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Her name's been bugging me since Tuesday. Kessler. I looked it up last night. Two thousand nine, Denver District Court. State case against an officer at a little state chartered bank. I worked the money side of it. She was the regulator's witness."</span> He smiled for the first time since Henry had known him. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Defense counsel asked her had she ever missed anything, in all those years. She said, 'Once.' Counsel was so surprised they forgot to ask when. The jury loved her."</span>`,
    `Henry wrote it in his notebook, word for word, with the year. Lou watched him do it and didn't say anything, which Henry was starting to understand was approval.`,
    `On the walk back down Sixteenth Street, he made a list in his head. He went into a drugstore for a notepad and came out with a box of green pens. He didn't ask himself why. At the office he wrote it on the whiteboard, under the shopping list nobody had erased. First the four steps and their years: the fifteenth, 1909. The eighteenth, 1969. The thirteenth, 2003. The seventeenth, 2026. Then, beside them, the markers in the claim pack. Leonard's controls report, signed in 2026. Hollins and Pike's opinion, as of December 31, 2026. Every document in the claim pack that said the capstone worked was a marker on a step, and every one had a year on it. Check the year, she'd written. He didn't know yet which year she meant. He knew where to start looking: at the people who were supposed to check.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Dot's Workpapers: who's in charge, and how they'd know`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Who's in charge, and of what`,
        body: [`Governance is the board's job, Lime Street, and management is management's. People blur the two all day long, usually on purpose. ISACA's COBIT framework draws the line cleanly. Governance evaluates the options, sets direction, and monitors whether the organization is getting where it said it would. Management plans, builds, runs, and monitors the work inside that direction. The board doesn't run the servers. It decides what the servers are for, how much risk it'll take with them, and how it'll find out if something goes wrong.`,
          `So the first thing I read at any company is the organization chart, with a pencil. I'm looking for one thing: can anyone who checks a process be overruled, hired, or fired by the person who runs it? Security that reports to the head of IT will be asked, now and then, to be reasonable. Internal audit that reports to the CFO for everything is the CFO's opinion on different letterhead. The people who give assurance need a line to the board that nobody in between can cut.`,
          `Boards do this work through committees. The audit committee oversees financial reporting, internal control, and the auditors, internal and external. It should meet the chief audit executive regularly, and sometimes with management out of the room. An IT strategy committee works at board level and advises the board on IT strategy and on how IT supports the business. An IT steering committee is a management group, senior people from the business and from IT, that sets priorities for IT projects, approves the big ones, and watches them. Know which is which. The exam does.`,
          `And IT strategy has to come from business strategy. An IT plan that could belong to any company in any industry isn't a strategy. Ask for the business plan and the IT plan and read them side by side. You'll know in ten minutes whether one was written with the other open on the desk.`],
        terms: [[`Governance and management`,`Governance evaluates, directs, and monitors. Management plans, builds, runs, and monitors within that direction. COBIT keeps them separate.`],[`Audit committee`,`A board committee that oversees financial reporting, internal control, and internal and external audit.`],[`IT strategy committee`,`A board level committee that advises the board on IT strategy and its alignment with the business.`],[`IT steering committee`,`A management level committee of business and IT leaders that prioritizes, approves, and monitors IT projects.`]],
        exam: `The board holds ultimate accountability for IT governance. An IT strategy committee is board level and advises on strategy. An IT steering committee is management level and runs project priorities. When a reporting line lets the person who runs a process control the people who check it, that's the concern to pick.` },
      { h: `Paper that's supposed to be true`,
        body: [`There's a ladder of documents, and you should know every rung. A policy is senior management's statement of intent, short and general: settlements will be properly authorized. A standard is a mandatory, specific requirement that makes the policy testable: two approvers above a set amount, and the second one didn't prepare the file. A procedure is the step by step for the person at the desk. A guideline is advice. If you can't test a sentence, it's probably policy. If it says "should consider," it's a guideline.`,
          `Policies can be written top down, from the business's goals pushed out through the company, which keeps them consistent. Or bottom up, from what operations actually do and what the risk assessments say, which keeps them realistic. Most companies do some of each. What matters to you is whether the rungs agree. Read the policy, then the standard, then the procedure, and check that each sentence made it down to the next document whole. Words get lost on the way down, and the ones that get lost are always the inconvenient ones. Then go and watch whether people actually do what the procedure says, because paper is a claim too.`,
          `Then check the year. A policy that says it's reviewed annually and carries a date from five years ago is a policy nobody owns. Systems change, people change, threats change, and the policy stays exactly as wise as it was the day it was signed.`,
          `Every company has exceptions, and that's fine. An exception is a decision to accept a risk, so treat it like one. Somebody with the authority to accept that risk signs it, and it shouldn't be the person who asked. It says why, what compensates, and when it ends. An exception with no end date isn't an exception anymore. It's the new policy, written by somebody who wasn't allowed to write policy.`,
          `The information security policy sits at the top of the security ladder. Senior management or the board approves it, everyone it covers is told about it, and it's reviewed when the business or the threats change. When I start on a company's security, it's the first thing I read, because every other security control is supposed to trace back to it. Frameworks such as ISO 27001 or the NIST Cybersecurity Framework help a company build that structure. They don't stand in for the company's own policy.`],
        terms: [[`Policy`,`A high level statement of management's intent and direction.`],[`Standard`,`A mandatory, specific requirement that supports a policy.`],[`Procedure`,`Step by step instructions for carrying out a task.`],[`Guideline`,`Recommended practice. Advisory, not mandatory.`],[`Policy exception`,`A documented, approved, time limited acceptance of risk where a policy or standard won't be met.`]],
        exam: `Mandatory, specific requirements live in standards. Once a procedure is documented and approved, the next step is testing whether it's followed. The information security policy states management's direction for security, needs senior management approval, and is the first document to review in a security audit.` },
      { h: `Risk has an owner`,
        body: [`Enterprise risk management is how a company decides which risks it will take, in the open, on purpose. The board sets the risk appetite: how much risk, and what kind, it's willing to take to reach its goals. Tolerance is the acceptable variation around that, for a particular objective. Go past it, and the risk is outside what the board agreed to. Management identifies risks, rates them, gives each one an owner, and keeps them in a risk register.`,
          `Ratings come in two kinds. Inherent risk is the exposure before controls. Residual risk is what's left after them. The gap between the two is a claim about controls, and you know what I think about claims. If the residual rating is low because a control works, find out who tested that control, and when.`,
          `You can rate risk qualitatively, with likelihood and impact on a scale of one to five, or quantitatively, in money. The exam likes the money version. Single loss expectancy, SLE, is what one event costs. The annual rate of occurrence, ARO, is how many times a year you expect it. Multiply them and you get the annualized loss expectancy, ALE. A two hundred thousand dollar loss you expect once every four years has an ARO of a quarter and an ALE of fifty thousand dollars a year. The arithmetic is easy. The inputs are guesses, so ask whose.`,
          `When a risk sits above appetite, there are four things to do with it. Avoid it by not doing the risky thing at all. Mitigate it with controls. Transfer or share it, usually with insurance or a contract. Or accept it, in writing, by somebody with the authority to. Insurance moves money. It doesn't move responsibility, and it doesn't make a control work.`,
          `And a risk assessment is good for as long as nothing important changes. When a company builds a new system, buys another company, or meets a new threat, the assessment gets done again.`],
        terms: [[`Risk appetite`,`The amount and type of risk an organization is willing to take in pursuit of its objectives. Set by the board.`],[`Residual risk`,`The risk that remains after controls are applied.`],[`ALE`,`Annualized loss expectancy: single loss expectancy times annual rate of occurrence.`],[`Risk responses`,`Avoid, mitigate, transfer or share, accept.`]],
        exam: `Insurance is risk transfer, and the board stays accountable for the risk. ALE equals SLE times ARO. A residual rating is only as good as the latest test of the controls it leans on.` },
      { h: `What the board gets told`,
        body: [`A board can't watch IT directly. It watches what it's shown, so somebody has to choose the measures, and that choice is half of governance. Two kinds matter. A key goal indicator tells you whether a goal was met, after the fact: fraud losses as a share of what was settled, say. A key performance indicator tells you whether a process is performing well enough to meet the goal, while there's still time to do something about it: reconciliation breaks found and cleared within a day. Goals lag. Performance leads.`,
          `A balanced scorecard puts several kinds of measure side by side, so nobody can win on one by losing on the others. Kaplan and Norton's original four perspectives were financial, customer, internal process, and learning and growth. IT versions usually measure contribution to the business, the users, operational excellence, and readiness for the future. It's a good tool. It's only as honest as the numbers in it.`,
          `So ask three things of every measure. What exactly does it count? Where do the numbers come from? Who checks them besides the people being measured? A reconciliation that runs every day and finds a problem every day will score a hundred percent on "completed." That's a true number about the wrong thing. And a scorecard drawn entirely from one system is that system's opinion of itself.`,
          `COBIT 4.1 graded processes on a maturity scale. COBIT 2019 calls this area performance management. It rates each process on capability levels from 0 to 5, and it uses maturity levels, on the same 0 to 5 scale, for focus areas and groups of processes. It also attaches example metrics to its enterprise goals and alignment goals, which is where the old KGI and KPI language went, though the exam still uses both names. Capability levels are useful for showing a board how far a process has come. They can't tell you whether it worked on a particular Tuesday.`],
        terms: [[`Key goal indicator (KGI)`,`A measure of whether a business or IT goal was achieved. Lagging.`],[`Key performance indicator (KPI)`,`A measure of how well a process is performing toward a goal. Leading.`],[`Balanced scorecard`,`A set of measures across several perspectives, linking IT performance to business goals.`],[`Capability and maturity levels`,`COBIT 2019 rates each process on capability levels from 0 to 5, and focus areas on maturity levels from 0 to 5. Both run from incomplete up to optimizing.`]],
        exam: `A KGI tells you whether the goal was achieved. A KPI tells you how the process is performing. The best measure for a board ties to the business goal and comes from a reliable source that someone independent can check.` },
      { h: `Writing it down for the people who need it`,
        body: [`A finding has four parts, and if one's missing it isn't finished. The condition is what you found. The criteria are what should have been: the policy, the standard, the law, the contract. The cause is why the gap exists, and it's the part people skip, because it usually leads back to somebody senior. The effect is what it means, in money or risk or both. Then the recommendation, aimed at the cause.`,
          `Your report says what you were asked to do, what you covered, the period, what you couldn't test and why, and what you found. Put a short summary at the top for the reader who'll only read the top, which is most of them. Anything significant shouldn't wait for the final report. Tell the right people as soon as it's supported, and say plainly that it's interim.`,
          `Before a report is final, you sit down with management and go through the findings. The main point of that exit meeting is to make sure your facts are right. You also check that your recommendations are realistic and agree on dates for the actions. It isn't a negotiation over your conclusions. Management gets to respond in writing, their response goes in the report beside your finding, and later you follow up to see whether they did what they said.`,
          `When anyone senior asks you to soften a finding, you write down that they asked, who, and when, and it goes in the file. I learned that the expensive way.`,
          `Then you keep the finding, if the evidence holds it up. Be fair to people. Get the cause right, so nobody carries a failure that belongs to the structure above them. Fair and soft are different words for a reason.`],
        terms: [[`Condition, criteria, cause, effect`,`The four parts of an audit finding: what is, what should be, why, and what it means.`],[`Exit meeting`,`A meeting with management before the report is final, to confirm the facts and discuss findings and responses.`],[`Management response`,`Management's written reply to a finding, included in the report, with planned actions and dates.`]],
        exam: `Significant findings are communicated promptly, in an interim communication if needed. Management's response belongs in the report beside the finding. The cause is the element that explains why the condition exists.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f3s1`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A2`, title:`Who answers to whom`, q:`What should Henry conclude from Meridian's organization chart?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The radiator was knocking like someone who wanted to be let in when Henry turned to page 12 of the claim pack. It was Meridian's organization chart. He'd looked at it on Tuesday. This time he read it.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian Clearing, organization chart (claim pack, page 12)">Board of Directors. Chairman: S. Kade, founder.<br>Audit Committee: three directors.<br>Chief Executive: S. Kade.<br>Chief Financial Officer: C. Wynn, reporting to the Chief Executive.<br>Reporting to the CFO: Settlement Operations; Finance and Reconciliation; Chief Information Officer; Internal Audit (L. Pratt).<br>Reporting to the CIO: Information Security; Database Administration; Security Monitoring (DOME).</prop>`,`He drew it again on the back of an envelope, with lines instead of boxes, the way Dot used to. All the lines went to the same place.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Conclude the chart is normal for a firm of Meridian's size.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He moves on. Later in the day he'll come back to the envelope, because every other finding on his list turns out to start there.`,
          why:`Size explains a small team. It doesn't explain one executive owning a process and everyone who checks it. Reporting lines are evidence about governance. Read them as evidence.`},
        {t:`Note that security reports to the CIO and sits under the IT it checks.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's a real concern, and he writes it down. Then he looks at the envelope again and sees that the CIO reports to the CFO too, along with the settlement desk, the reconciliation team, and internal audit. The security line is one branch of a bigger tree.`,
          why:`Security reporting into IT is a classic independence concern, and you're right to note it. But it isn't the biggest one on this chart. Look for the point where the process and all of its checks meet.`},
        {t:`Note that the settlement process and all its checks report to the CFO.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it at the top of a clean page. Settlement operations, reconciliation, IT, security, database administration, internal audit: every one of them rolls up to Carla Wynn. The people who run the capstone and the people who check it share a boss. Underneath he writes the question that follows: what did the board hear about any of it, and from whom?`,
          why:`Good governance keeps the people who run a process apart from the people who give assurance over it, and gives the assurance functions a line to the board. When one executive owns the process, the systems, security, and internal audit, nobody independent of that executive reports on the key control.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard Pratt how the reporting lines work in practice.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Leonard explains, warmly and at length, that Carla has an open door. It's a nice thing to say about a boss. It isn't evidence about governance.`,
          why:`Inquiry of someone inside the structure you're assessing is the weakest evidence you can get, and Leonard sits inside it. Get the documents that show who actually reports to whom.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A2`, title:`Eight quarters`, q:`Internal audit wasn't at any of eight audit committee meetings in two years. What follows for Henry's work?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Just after nine, Vellacott's counsel forwarded what he'd asked for on Wednesday: Meridian's board and audit committee minutes for 2025 and 2026. Eight quarters. He read every page, then made a table of them on a legal pad.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Henry's notes: audit committee minutes, Q1 2025 to Q4 2026">Meetings held: 8 of 8.<br>Present every time: three directors; S. Kade (Chairman, by invitation); C. Wynn (CFO).<br>Internal audit: not present at any meeting, not on any agenda.<br>Session without management present: none recorded.<br>IT or security on the agenda: once, Q2 2025, "DOME dashboard: noted."</prop>`,`One name sat in all eight meetings, by invitation. The chairman, who was also the chief executive, who was also the man the CFO reported to. Henry underlined it once and turned the page.`,(G.trustedia?`On Tuesday he'd cut back his own controls testing on the strength of Leonard's report, and told himself he'd come back to it. Here it was, coming back to him. For two years, the only person who'd told Meridian's audit committee anything about controls was the CFO whose systems they were.`:`On Tuesday he'd written that his reliance on Leonard's work was limited, pending review. Here was the review. For two years, the only person who'd told Meridian's audit committee anything about controls was the CFO whose systems they were.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Conclude the board can't have known of any weakness in dual authorization.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He writes it, reads it back, and crosses it out. The minutes show what the committee was told. They don't show what any director knew.`,
          why:`The minutes are evidence of what was reported and recorded. Drawing a conclusion about what people knew goes beyond that evidence. State what the minutes show.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel if internal audit reported in writing and wait to hear.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel says she'll ask, and that Meridian will take a week. It's a fair question. In the meantime he has eight quarters of minutes and a charter signed by the CFO, and he hasn't written either of them down as a finding.`,
          why:`Checking for other evidence is reasonable, and you can ask when you confirm the facts. But the minutes and the charter already support the finding. Record what they show now, and let the confirmation correct it if it's wrong.`},
        {t:`Record the gap and give internal audit's 2026 report no weight.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it plainly: no meeting between internal audit and the audit committee in eight quarters, no private session, a charter approved by the CFO alone. Then, in the reliance column for Leonard's 2026 report, he crosses out what he'd written there on Tuesday and writes "none." He doesn't enjoy it. Leonard has been nothing but helpful. That was rather the problem.`,
          why:`An audit committee should oversee internal audit and meet the chief audit executive, including without management present. A function that never reaches those charged with governance has no independent line to them, and its work can't be relied on without one.`},
        {t:`Ring the audit committee chair and ask why internal audit never came.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He finds a number for the chair in the minutes and has it half dialed before he thinks about Agent Obi, the FBI's preservation letter, and Vellacott's counsel. He puts the phone down. It was a short call to nowhere, and the worst kind of short call.`,
          why:`Contacting the insured's directors around counsel, during a claim and a federal investigation, risks the evidence and the claim's process. Requests go through the channel the engagement sets.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s3`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A3`, title:`Three documents`, q:`Three documents set Meridian's authorization rules. How should Henry use them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Next he opened the folder Leonard had sent on Wednesday under the subject line <b>Everything you need on authorization!</b> It held three documents. He laid them out side by side on the desk, oldest on the left.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement authorization: the three documents">Settlement Authorization Policy. Approved by the Board, March 2019. "All outgoing settlements shall be authorized in accordance with the Settlement Authorization Standard. This policy is reviewed annually."<br><br>Settlement Authorization Standard. Approved by the CFO, 2019. "Settlement files above $10,000 require approval by a second authorized user who did not prepare the file."<br><br>Desk Procedure SO-14, revised January 2026. "Step 6. Second approver confirms the file total and releases."</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Test against the board's policy and set the other two aside.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He tries to write a test against "properly authorized" and gives up after twenty minutes. Every file Meridian ever sent could be called properly authorized by somebody.`,
          why:`A policy states intent. It's too general to test against. The specific, mandatory requirement you can test is in the standard.`},
        {t:`Use the desk procedure staff follow as his criteria.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's what staff follow, and he'll need it. But the procedure only asks the second approver to confirm a total. If he tests against that, he'll be testing whether Meridian did what its own shortened instructions said.`,
          why:`The procedure tells you what people were told to do, which matters. But your criteria should be the mandatory requirement. Testing only against a procedure can bake its gaps into your conclusion.`},
        {t:`Trace the policy down to the procedure and note where it changes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The standard says the second approver must be someone who didn't prepare the file. The procedure, revised in January 2026, says only that the second approver confirms the total and releases. The words about who may approve didn't make it down the ladder. Then the years: a policy that requires an annual review, approved in March 2019. Reviews were due every March from 2020 to 2026. That's seven at least, and the file holds none. He writes <b>check the year</b> in the margin, and then feels slightly foolish, and leaves it.`,
          why:`Policies, standards, and procedures should agree, from intent down to the steps at the desk. Trace each requirement down the ladder and note where it changes, and check each document's approval and review dates against what it says about itself.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian to rewrite all three before he tests anything.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It would make next year tidier. It does nothing for the question of what the documents said, and what people did, in the first week of March.`,
          why:`Recommendations come after you've established the facts. The claim turns on what was in force and what happened, so test against the documents as they were.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s4`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A3`, title:`No end date`, q:`An open 2022 exception suspended reviews of who can approve settlements. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The last thing in Leonard's folder was Meridian's policy exception register, a spreadsheet of nineteen rows going back to 2019. Most were dull: a laptop standard waived for a contractor, a password rule relaxed for a printer. One wasn't.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Policy exception register (extract)">EX-2022-07. Quarterly review of the authorized approver list, Settlement Authorization Standard section 4: suspended for Q3 and Q4 2022 due to staffing.<br>Requested by: Settlement Operations. Approved by: C. Wynn, CFO.<br>Compensating control: none stated. Expiry: none stated. Status: OPEN.</prop>`,`He checked the review log for the approver list. The last review was dated June 2022.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Treat the CFO's approval as closing the matter and move on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He moves on to the next row. The list of people allowed to approve settlements has gone unreviewed for nearly five years, and his file says nothing about it.`,
          why:`Approval doesn't turn an exception into compliance. It records a decision to accept a risk, and that decision still has to be made by the right person, for a stated time, with something compensating.`},
        {t:`Ask for the March 5 approver list and check it against HR records.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`It's a good test, and he puts it on the request list. It'll tell him who could approve on March 5. It won't tell anyone how a quarterly control was switched off for nearly five years with a single signature.`,
          why:`Testing the list directly is useful work. But the exception itself is a governance failure that needs recording: no expiry, no compensating control, and nothing showing that anyone weighed the risk.`},
        {t:`Log a waiver with no end date or compensating control.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it as a finding: a quarterly review of who may approve settlements, suspended in 2022 for two quarters, never restarted, no end date, nothing to compensate, signed off by the CFO whose department asked for it. Then he adds the approver list to the request list as well, because the finding tells him exactly where to test next.`,
          why:`An exception is a risk acceptance. It's approved by the risk owner with the authority to accept it, not by the person who asked, and it carries a stated reason, a compensating control, and an end date. An exception without an end date has quietly become the policy.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard's team to confirm in writing that reviews restarted.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Leonard confirms within the hour that reviews "resumed on a business as usual basis." There are no dates and no documents attached.`,
          why:`A written assertion from the auditee isn't evidence that a control ran. Ask for the records of the reviews themselves: dates, reviewers, and what changed.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A5`, title:`Residual: low`, q:`Meridian's register scores its settlement fraud risk a five, last reviewed years ago. How should Henry read it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Meridian's risk register was at the back of the Q4 2026 board pack, in an appendix nobody had bothered to number. Row seven was the one that mattered.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian Clearing, enterprise risk register, row R-07">Risk: unauthorized or fraudulent settlement. Owner: CFO.<br>Inherent: likelihood 4, impact 5, score 20.<br>Key control: dual authorization (assessed effective, 2019 control assessment).<br>Residual: likelihood 1, impact 5, score 5 (LOW).<br>Board appetite: residual score above 8 requires a treatment plan reported to the board.<br>Last reviewed: 2019. Next review: 2020.</prop>`,`The settlement platform described in the claim pack had been built in 2019. He wrote a question he couldn't answer yet. Before, or after?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the board's rating as its own call on a risk it owns.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He writes "residual: low, per Meridian." It's their risk, and that was their rating, eight years ago.`,
          why:`A residual rating is a conclusion about controls. If its basis is old or untested, the rating is too. The board owns the risk, and you still evaluate the evidence behind what it was told.`},
        {t:`Ask the CFO, as the risk's owner, for her current view of the rating.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`He puts it on the list for counsel. Carla Wynn will have a view, and he'll want it on record. It'll be an opinion from the person whose department the control sits in, about a test done in 2019.`,
          why:`Asking the owner is fine as a step, but her view is an assertion. The weakness is in the basis: a rating that rests on a control test nobody has repeated.`},
        {t:`Treat the low rating as unsupported until the control is retested.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it down. The residual score of 5 rests on a control assessment from 2019, never repeated, for a risk that scores 20 without the control. That score sits under the board's line of 8, so the board was never shown a treatment plan, and never asked for one. The register was due for review in 2020. He circles 2019 in green, then stops and looks at the pen.`,
          why:`Residual risk depends on controls working, and controls have to be retested, especially when systems change. A rating whose basis is years old isn't evidence of current risk. Record the gap and the reason, and don't substitute your own rating as fact.`},
        {t:`Re-rate the risk as high and tell Giles the board was negligent.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Giles would love it. Meridian's lawyers would love it more, because Henry has no test of his own to support a new rating, and "negligent" is a legal word he isn't qualified to use.`,
          why:`Swapping management's untested rating for your own untested one doesn't help. Report what the evidence shows about the basis of the rating, and leave the legal conclusions to the people qualified to draw them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s6`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A5`, title:`Somebody else's problem`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 11:05 Giles rang from a taxi. It was five past six in London, and he sounded as if the taxi were going somewhere he was looking forward to.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Quick thought for your view, Henry. Meridian bought our policy, so the risk's been transferred, hasn't it? Their board can rate things however it likes. Once they've insured it, it's ours. So their governance is rather beside the point, surely."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Agreed. Once it's insured, how they managed it is our concern, not theirs."`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Splendid, one less section."</span> Henry hangs up and looks at row R-07 and realizes he's just agreed to leave out the reason the risk was rated low.`,
          why:`Insurance transfers part of the financial impact. It doesn't transfer the duty to manage the risk, and it doesn't make a control work. How the insured governed the risk is exactly what you're assessing.`},
        {t:`"Insurance moves the cost. Their board still owned the risk and the control."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"And we wrote the policy on what they told us about that control. If their own board was rating it on a 2019 test, that matters to us twice."</span> There's a pause with traffic in it. <span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Fair. One paragraph. Plain English, please. The board's had a week of acronyms."</span>`,
          why:`Risk transfer, usually through insurance, shares the financial impact. Accountability for the risk and for the controls stays with the organization's board and management. That's why governance still matters after a risk is insured.`},
        {t:`"Then we can decline. A board that rated it low accepted the risk."`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Can we? On what wording?"</span> Henry can't find one. Rating a risk low isn't the same as accepting it, and neither one is an exclusion in the policy.`,
          why:`A low rating is an assessment. Acceptance is a formal decision by someone with authority. Treating one as the other confuses risk management terms, and coverage turns on the policy wording.`},
        {t:`"Partly. It matters only if the proposal form misstated their controls."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Well, did it?"</span> Henry doesn't know yet. It's a sharp point, and a narrow one, and it leaves out everything their board was supposed to be doing in the meantime.`,
          why:`What the insured represented about its controls matters a great deal. But governance isn't only relevant through the proposal form. The board kept accountability for the risk, and that's part of the picture.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s7`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B3`, title:`All green`, q:`Every box on Meridian's IT scorecard is green, all sourced from DOME. How should Henry use it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Every director had read this page. You could tell from the coffee ring on Meridian's scanned copy. One page. Four measures. Eight quarters of them, and every box green.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Board pack, IT performance scorecard, Q4 2026">Settlement platform availability: 99.97% (target 99.9%).<br>Daily reconciliations completed: 100% (target 100%).<br>Security alerts triaged within service level: 100% (target 98%).<br>Critical patches applied within 30 days: 96% (target 95%).<br>Source for all measures: DOME. Prepared by: IT.</prop>`,`He read the footer twice. Don't trust the dome.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take the scorecard as evidence that the controls ran in 2026.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`He writes "IT performance satisfactory per board reporting." Four green boxes, and not one of them mentions a second approver.`,
          why:`A scorecard is management's report of its own performance. Unless you know what each measure counts, where it comes from, and who checks it, it isn't evidence that a particular control worked.`},
        {t:`Flag eight quarters of all green as too good to be true.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`He writes "suspiciously perfect" and underlines it. It's a fair instinct. It's also a feeling, and Priyanka would send it straight back.`,
          why:`Skepticism is right, but "too good" isn't a finding. Find out what the measures actually count and where they come from. That's where the real problem shows.`},
        {t:`Ask for a DOME login and check the numbers at the source.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Counsel's reply arrives within ten minutes and is one word longer than no.`,
          why:`Signing in to the auditee's production monitoring system puts you inside the evidence. Ask for defined extracts through the proper channel.`},
        {t:`Ask how each measure is defined and who checks the figures.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes the questions beside each line. The answers are already mostly on the page. All four come from DOME, all four were prepared by IT, and nobody outside IT checks them. And none of them measures whether the key control did its job. "Reconciliations completed" counts that the reconciliation ran. It doesn't say what it found.`,
          why:`Performance measures are only useful if they're relevant to the goal, clearly defined, and reliably sourced, with someone independent checking them. A scorecard drawn from one system and prepared by the team it measures is that system's view of itself.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s8`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B3`, title:`Completed`, q:`The reconciliation sheets show three days completed, two with differences carried forward. What should Henry conclude?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Agent Obi rang at half past twelve to say the bureau's formal request for the claim file had gone to Vellacott's counsel that morning. It took her about ninety seconds. Then, in the same even voice, <span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"You were Intelligence Corps."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Is that in a file somewhere?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"It's in how you stood on your landing on Tuesday. Like you'd already counted the exits."</span> She rang off before he'd decided whether it was a compliment.`,`The reconciliations arrived after lunch, the ones he'd put at the top of his list on Tuesday. Meridian had sent the sign off sheets for the week of the event.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Daily settlement reconciliation against the Federal Reserve record, sign off sheets">Tue, Mar 2 (settlements of Mar 1): completed 7:41 AM. Difference: none.<br>Wed, Mar 3 (settlements of Mar 2): completed 7:44 AM. Difference: YES. Disposition: timing difference, carried forward. Supervisor's initials: B.N.<br>Thu, Mar 4 (settlements of Mar 3): completed 7:39 AM. Difference: YES. Disposition: timing difference, carried forward. Supervisor's initials: B.N.<br>Fri, Mar 5 (settlements of Mar 4): not performed. Incident declared 4:40 AM.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Conclude the detective control worked on every day it ran.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It ran. That's what the board was told, a hundred percent. He writes "detective control operated," and the sentence is true and leaves out the only part that matters.`,
          why:`A control that detects a problem and then has its result set aside hasn't done its job. Measure the outcome: what it found, and what was done about it.`},
        {t:`Wait for finance to explain each break in writing before concluding.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He sends the request through counsel. The explanations will be useful. They'll also be written by the department that carried the breaks forward, about why it carried them forward.`,
          why:`Asking for explanations is fair, and you'll need them. But you can already state what the sheets show, and the gap between what was measured and what mattered is yours to report.`},
        {t:`Conclude the measure counted runs while the breaks were waved on.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`He writes it in two lines. The reconciliation ran and found a difference on March 3 and again on March 4, and both times the difference was called timing and carried forward, with no escalation. The board's measure counted only that it ran. Then he sits back. The control Dot taught him to look for had done exactly what it was for, twice, and somebody had initialed it away.`,
          why:`A KPI that counts completion can be perfect while the process fails at its purpose. The measure that matters here is breaks found and resolved, and how. Report the gap between what was measured and what the board needed to know.`},
        {t:`Recommend Meridian add a board measure for breaks and their outcome.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's a good measure. He writes it down, and realizes he's designed next year's scorecard before writing up what happened on March 3.`,
          why:`Recommendations follow findings. First record what the evidence shows about this year's control and this year's reporting.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s9`, domain:`Auditing Process`, task:`1B5`, title:`Four parts`, q:`Henry has three hours to draft Giles's interim view. How should its conclusions be laid out?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He started the interim view itself at ten past two. He'd promised it to Giles by five, Denver time, so it would be the first thing Giles read in the morning. He had a blank page and three hours, and he had a great many things he believed.`,(G.cherrypicked?`The sampling section was the one he'd been avoiding. Forty rows, chosen on Wednesday night because they looked like fraud, and a neat paragraph about them that didn't say so. He'd have to decide what to do with it before anyone in London read it.`:`The sampling section was easy. Two strata, random selection, seeds written down. It was the governance section that worried him, because it was about people.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lead with his view that the claim is a fraud, and say why.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`It reads beautifully, and it's a theory. On Monday it'll be read aloud to a board, and by Tuesday it'll be in a letter from Meridian's lawyers.`,
          why:`An interim report states what's supported and labels what isn't. Leading with an unproven conclusion puts your words ahead of your evidence and exposes your client.`},
        {t:`Keep it short and leave out whatever he hasn't tested.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's short. It also makes the work look further along than it is, because nothing in it says what he hasn't looked at.`,
          why:`A report has to state its scope and its limitations: what wasn't tested, and why. Leaving them out makes the conclusions look stronger than they are.`},
        {t:`List the facts plainly and save conclusions for the final report.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's accurate and careful, and Giles will finish it with no idea why any of it matters.`,
          why:`Facts without cause and effect leave the reader to guess what they mean. A finding should say what it means and why it happened, as far as the evidence goes.`},
        {t:`Set out each finding by condition, criteria, cause and effect.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes the first one as Dot would have. Condition: internal audit didn't attend or report to the audit committee in eight quarters. Criteria: an audit charter approved by those charged with governance, and a functional line to them. Cause: a charter approved only by the CFO, and internal audit reporting to the CFO for everything. Effect: no independent assurance to Meridian's board over the key control, and Vellacott can't rely on internal audit's 2026 report. Then a paragraph headed <b>Not yet tested</b>, which is longer than he'd like and exactly as long as it needs to be.`,
          why:`A complete finding states the condition, the criteria, the cause, and the effect. The report also states its limitations, so readers know how far the conclusions go.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s10`, domain:`Auditing Process`, task:`1B5`, title:`A favor`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 3:05 his phone showed a Meridian number. It was Leonard Pratt, and he wasn't cheerful.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Mr. Ashby. I hear you asked for the audit committee minutes. I can guess what you found."</span> A breath. <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Off the record. If your report says internal audit never met the committee, that's my name on it. I asked to go. Twice. In writing. I kept the emails, for whatever that's worth. Carla said the committee gets what it needs from the CFO's office. I've got a mortgage in Arvada and a boss who reads everything. Could it say 'reporting lines need review'? It's the same thing. It's just kinder."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Soften the wording to "reporting lines need review" as he asks.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`He changes it. "Reporting lines need review." It's kinder. It's also a sentence any reader can agree with without learning anything, and the file now says nothing about why it changed.`,
          why:`Softening a supported finding at the request of the auditee changes what the reader learns. Fairness to an individual comes from getting the cause right, and the wording of the condition stays.`},
        {t:`Keep the finding and add that Leonard says he asked twice.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's a decent instinct. It also puts an unverified phone call into a written finding, where it reads as fact.`,
          why:`What Leonard says may be true, and it may matter to the cause. Get it in writing, through the proper channel, as management's response, and keep your finding to what the evidence shows.`},
        {t:`Keep the finding and note in the file who asked to soften it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"The finding stays,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"It's about the structure. The cause in it is the charter and the reporting line, and neither of those is yours. Send me those emails through counsel, and they'll go in beside the finding as management's response, in your own words."</span> A long quiet. <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Okay,"</span> Leonard says. <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Okay. I can do that."</span> After the call Henry writes in the file: <b>3:05 PM. L. Pratt, by phone, asked that the finding on audit committee reporting be reworded as "reporting lines need review." Declined. Invited a written response through counsel, with his emails.</b>`,
          why:`When someone asks you to soften a supported finding, keep it, and document who asked, when, and what you decided. Fairness comes through an accurate cause and a written management response included in the report.`},
        {t:`Hold the finding back until Leonard can raise it with the board.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Leonard thanks him three times. The interim view goes to London without its most important governance finding, on the word of the man it's about.`,
          why:`Delaying a significant finding at the auditee's request isn't timely reporting. The appropriate parties need it when it's supported.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s11`, domain:`Auditing Process`, task:`1B5`, title:`One word`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 3:40 Giles rang again. It was twenty to eleven in London, and he was at home, and he didn't sound as though the evening had gone the way the taxi had promised.`,...(G.hidconflict
        ?[`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Meridian's general counsel wrote to our general counsel this afternoon. A letter about our investigator's relationship with the late examiner named in the notice. Your card was in her hand, Henry. I learned it from them."</span> The quiet on the line was worse than shouting. <span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Compliance will want a word on Monday. Until then you're still on it, because there's nobody else. Now. The board."</span>`]
        :[`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Meridian's counsel tried to make something of you and the examiner today. Our general counsel pointed them at the disclosure note on your file from Tuesday. It lasted about four minutes. That's what notes are for. Now. The board."</span>`]),`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"On Monday they'll ask me one thing. Do we pay. I need one word, Henry. Fraud, or not fraud?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Fraud. The settlements just under the line make it obvious."`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Good. That's what I'll say."</span> Henry spends the next twenty minutes trying to walk it back, and the word stays in Giles's head anyway.`,
          why:`A pattern isn't a conclusion. Communicating more certainty than the evidence supports misleads the reader, and on a 300 million dollar claim it can't be taken back.`},
        {t:`"Not fraud, for now. It's safer to say less until we're sure."`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Giles writes it down. It's a conclusion too, and a softer one, and it's just as unsupported.`,
          why:`"Not fraud" is also a conclusion. If you don't know, say so, and say what you do know and when you'll know more.`},
        {t:`"I can't give you one word. The interim view explains why."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Then I'll read it,"</span> Giles says, a little coldly. It's honest. It isn't much help to a man who has to stand up in front of a board.`,
          why:`Declining to overstate is right. But a report has to serve its reader. Give the decision maker what is known, what isn't, and when, in a form they can use.`},
        {t:`"Not yet. I can tell you what we know and when we'll know the rest."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He gives it in under a minute. What's known: the governance gaps, the reconciliation breaks waved through, the 2,906 under the line. What isn't: why, and by whom. When: the Federal Reserve totals Monday, sample results the week after next. <span class="said" data-who="Giles">"That's three things, not one word."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"It's one page."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Then put a box at the top of it with what I say to the board, and I'll say exactly that."</span>`,
          why:`Good reporting is clear, timely, and fitted to the reader. An executive summary that states what's supported, what's open, and when it will close gives a decision maker something true to say.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s12`, domain:`Auditing Process`, task:`1B5`, title:`Facts first`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By twenty past four the interim view was nearly done. Three of its facts were about Meridian's own records: the audit committee minutes, the charter's approver, and the dates on the authorization policy. If any of them were wrong, he'd rather know before Giles read them to a board.`,`Vellacott's counsel rang to ask if he wanted anything sent to Meridian before the weekend.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Send nothing and treat Meridian's own records as confirmed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Monday morning Meridian's counsel points out that the 2019 policy was re-approved in a board resolution that isn't in the minutes he received. There turns out to be no such resolution. It still costs him a day proving that.`,
          why:`Records can be incomplete, and you may have misread them. Confirming factual findings with management before they're final protects the accuracy of the report.`},
        {t:`Ring Leonard and read him the three facts to check them.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Leonard confirms all three on the phone, sounding tired. Nothing's in writing, and Leonard isn't the person who can speak for the board's records.`,
          why:`Confirming facts is the right idea. Do it in writing, through the agreed channel, with someone who can speak for the records, so the confirmation itself is evidence.`},
        {t:`Send Meridian the whole interim view and ask for comment.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`Counsel refuses, which is the only reason it doesn't happen. The view includes the 2,906 settlements and the reconciliation breaks. Sending it would have told Meridian exactly where the insurer was looking, during a federal investigation.`,
          why:`Discuss findings with management to confirm facts, but don't hand the auditee your work on suspected fraud before the evidence is secured and the right parties have it.`},
        {t:`Send only the governance facts through counsel to confirm.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Counsel sends a one page letter at 4:41: three statements of fact, each with its source, and a request to confirm or correct them in writing by Wednesday. Nothing about settlements, reconciliations, or 3:12 AM. Henry marks those three lines in the interim view <b>facts subject to confirmation</b> and sends it to Giles at 5:02.`,
          why:`The main purpose of discussing findings with management before they're final is to confirm the facts. Do it in writing, through the agreed channel, and limit it to what management needs to check, keeping anything that could prejudice an investigation out of it.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f3s5`, title:`What the board was told`,
     setup:[`Meridian's risk register rates likelihood and impact from 1 to 5 and scores a risk by multiplying them. Row R-07 rates unauthorized settlement at likelihood 4 and impact 5 before controls, and likelihood 1 and impact 5 after dual authorization. The board wants a treatment plan for any residual score above 8. A 2019 annex to the register estimated a single loss of $5,000,000 at an annual rate of occurrence of 0.02. The claim says $41,000,000 went out in fraudulent settlements.`],
     fields:[{label:`Inherent risk score`,answer:20,tol:0},{label:`Residual risk score as registered`,answer:5,tol:0},{label:`Annualized loss expectancy in the 2019 annex, in dollars`,answer:100000,tol:0},{label:`Claimed fraud as a multiple of that ALE`,answer:410,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Inherent = likelihood × impact = 4 × 5 = 20, near the top of a 25 point scale.`,`Residual = 1 × 5 = 5. That's under the board's line of 8, so no treatment plan was ever reported. The whole drop from 20 to 5 rests on one control, assessed once, in 2019.`,`ALE = SLE × ARO = $5,000,000 × 0.02 = $100,000 a year. In plain words: a five million dollar loss about once every fifty years.`,`$41,000,000 ÷ $100,000 = 410. The claimed fraud is 410 years of the loss the 2019 annex expected. That doesn't prove the inputs were wrong. It tells you to ask whose guesses they were, and when anyone last checked them.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f3s3`, title:`Which rung?`, intro:`Dot kept a ladder drawn on the inside cover of her notebook with four rungs labeled. Put each sentence from Meridian's paperwork on the right rung.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`All outgoing settlements shall be properly authorized.`, choices:[`Policy`,`Standard`,`Procedure`,`Guideline`], answer:0, why:`A short, general statement of intent from the top. That's policy. You can't test it on its own.`},
      {prompt:`Settlement files above $10,000 require approval by a second user who did not prepare the file.`, choices:[`Policy`,`Standard`,`Procedure`,`Guideline`], answer:1, why:`Mandatory and specific enough to test. A standard.`},
      {prompt:`Step 6. Open the release queue, confirm the file total, and click Release.`, choices:[`Policy`,`Standard`,`Procedure`,`Guideline`], answer:2, why:`Step by step instructions for the person at the desk. A procedure.`},
      {prompt:`Approvers should consider phoning the merchant if a file looks unusual.`, choices:[`Policy`,`Standard`,`Procedure`,`Guideline`], answer:3, why:`"Should consider" is advice. A guideline.`},
      {prompt:`Passwords for settlement systems must be at least 14 characters long.`, choices:[`Policy`,`Standard`,`Procedure`,`Guideline`], answer:1, why:`A specific, mandatory requirement. A standard.`},
      {prompt:`Meridian will protect the information its customers entrust to it.`, choices:[`Policy`,`Standard`,`Procedure`,`Guideline`], answer:0, why:`A statement of management's intent for security. Policy, and the kind that sits at the top of an information security policy.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Who holds ULTIMATE accountability for the governance of an organization's IT?`, opts:[`The chief information officer`,`The IT steering committee`,`The board of directors`,`The chief audit executive`], a:2, why:`Governance, including IT governance, is the board's responsibility. Management, including the CIO and the steering committee, carries it out within the board's direction.`, task:`2A2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Which BEST describes an IT strategy committee?`, opts:[`A board level group that advises on IT strategy`,`A management group that ranks IT projects`,`An IT team that writes technical standards`,`An audit team that tests IT general controls`], a:0, why:`The IT strategy committee works at board level and advises the board on IT strategy and alignment. Ranking and overseeing projects is the IT steering committee's job, which is management level.`, task:`2A2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Where should a mandatory, specific requirement such as "two approvers above $10,000" be set out?`, opts:[`In a high level policy`,`In a standard`,`In a guideline`,`In a job description`], a:1, why:`Standards hold mandatory, specific requirements that support a policy. Policies state intent, guidelines advise, and job descriptions describe roles.`, task:`2A3`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Which of these is a CORRECTIVE control?`, opts:[`Two people approve each payment file`,`Restoring data from backup after an error`,`A daily review of failed sign in attempts`,`A badge reader on the server room door`], a:1, why:`Corrective controls fix the damage after an event, and restoring from backup does that. Dual approval and the badge reader are preventive. Reviewing failed sign ins is detective. Review of chapter 1.`, task:`1A4`, domain:`Auditing Process`},
    {q:`A department asks for an exception to an information security standard. Which is MOST important?`, opts:[`The department head signs it off`,`It is logged in the ticket system`,`A risk owner accepts it, with an end date`,`The auditors hear about it next audit`], a:2, why:`An exception is a risk acceptance. It's accepted by the risk owner with the authority to do so, not by the person who asked, for a stated period, with a reason and compensating controls.`, task:`2A3`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`An organization buys insurance to cover losses from a cyber attack. Which risk response is this?`, opts:[`Risk avoidance`,`Risk mitigation`,`Risk acceptance`,`Risk transfer`], a:3, why:`Insurance transfers, or shares, part of the financial impact. The organization stays accountable for managing the risk.`, task:`2A5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`An event would cost $200,000 each time and is expected once every four years. What is the annualized loss expectancy?`, opts:[`$800,000`,`$200,000`,`$50,000`,`$25,000`], a:2, why:`ARO = 1 ÷ 4 = 0.25. ALE = SLE × ARO = $200,000 × 0.25 = $50,000 a year. $800,000 multiplies by four instead of dividing.`, task:`2A5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`To confirm that daily reconciliations were performed correctly, which evidence is MOST reliable?`, opts:[`The supervisor's signed sign off sheets`,`The auditor re-performing a sample of them`,`The finance team's written description`,`The procedure that lists the steps`], a:1, why:`Re-performance by the auditor is generally the most reliable evidence. Sign off sheets show someone initialed a page, and descriptions and procedures show what was meant to happen. Review of chapter 1.`, task:`1B3`, domain:`Auditing Process`},
    {q:`Which measure would tell a board MOST about whether a daily reconciliation control is achieving its purpose?`, opts:[`Reconciliations completed on schedule`,`Breaks found and cleared within a day`,`Staff trained on the reconciliation`,`Uptime of the reconciliation system`], a:1, why:`The reconciliation exists to find and resolve differences. Breaks found and cleared measures that purpose. Completion, training, and uptime measure activity around it.`, task:`2B3`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Which of these is a key goal indicator (KGI) rather than a key performance indicator (KPI)?`, opts:[`Fraud losses as a share of value settled`,`Share of reconciliations done by 9 AM`,`Average hours to clear a reconciliation break`,`Share of approvers trained this quarter`], a:0, why:`A KGI measures whether the goal was achieved, after the fact. Fraud losses measure the outcome. The others measure how well processes are performing toward it, which makes them KPIs.`, task:`2B3`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`During fieldwork an IS auditor finds a significant control weakness. The final report is six weeks away. What should the auditor do?`, opts:[`Hold it for the final report`,`Fix it with the IT team first`,`Mention it to the system owner informally`,`Report it promptly to the right people`], a:3, why:`Significant findings shouldn't wait for the final report. Communicate them promptly, in an interim communication, to the appropriate parties.`, task:`1B5`, domain:`Auditing Process`},
    {q:`Where does management's response to an audit finding BELONG?`, opts:[`In a separate letter kept by management`,`In the auditor's private notes only`,`In the report, beside the finding`,`Nowhere, if management disagrees`], a:2, why:`Management's response, with planned actions and dates, is included in the report next to the finding, including when management disagrees.`, task:`1B5`, domain:`Auditing Process`},
    {q:`Which element of an audit finding explains WHY the condition exists?`, opts:[`Criteria`,`Effect`,`Condition`,`Cause`], a:3, why:`Condition is what was found, criteria is what should be, cause is why the gap exists, and effect is what it means.`, task:`1B5`, domain:`Auditing Process`},
    {q:`What is the PRIMARY role of an information security policy?`, opts:[`To state management's direction for security`,`To list the firewall rules for each zone`,`To describe the steps for a password reset`,`To record security incidents and closures`], a:0, why:`The policy states management's intent and direction for security. Rules, steps, and records belong in standards, procedures, and logs beneath it.`, task:`5A1`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`When starting a review of an organization's information security program, what should the IS auditor review FIRST?`, opts:[`Firewall rule sets`,`Recent incident logs`,`User access lists`,`The security policy`], a:3, why:`The information security policy sets the direction every other control should trace back to, so it's the starting point for the review.`, task:`5A1`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const missed=!!G.missedfair,picked=!!G.cherrypicked,P=s.picks||{};
    return [
    `The interim view went at 5:02. ${P[10]===3?`It was one page, with a box at the top.`:`It was one page. Whatever he'd said to Giles on the phone, the page said only what he could support.`}`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Interim view, Meridian Clearing claim, Friday, March 12, 2027 (not an opinion)">Governance: no one independent of the CFO reported to Meridian's board on the key control in 2025 or 2026. ${P[11]===3?`Facts sent to Meridian through counsel for confirmation.`:`Facts drawn from Meridian's own records.`}<br>Detective control: the daily reconciliation found differences on March 3 and March 4. Both were carried forward as timing differences.<br>Data: 2,906 claimed settlements sit just under the second approver line. Cause not yet known.<br>Next: Federal Reserve totals Monday; sample results the week of March 22.</prop>`,
    ...(picked
      ?[`Priyanka answered from London at 5:40, which was twenty to one in the morning there, so she'd either been waiting up or couldn't sleep. <b>Page two. Your forty rows. How were they chosen? It doesn't say.</b> He looked at the paragraph for a long time. Then he wrote back the truth, that he'd chosen them because they looked like what he expected, and that he'd draw a proper sample on Monday. <b>Good,</b> she sent. <b>Put that sentence in the file too.</b>`]
      :[]),
    (s.score>=28
      ?`He sat with the window cracked and the bicycle shop's radio coming up through the floor, and went over the day in order. A policy nobody had reviewed since 2019. An exception that never ended. A risk rated on a 2019 test. Two breaks, initialed away. A man in Arvada asking for kinder words. None of it said who had done what. All of it said that nobody whose job was to look had been allowed to.`
      :`He went over the day in order, the way Dot would have, and didn't love all of it. Some of the calls, she'd have circled. The finding was in the file anyway, under the mistakes, and the mistakes were in the file too, which was something.`),
    `At 6:05 his phone lit up. Laura.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Laura" time="6:05 PM">Can we get coffee Monday evening? Pearl Street, the place by the bookshop, 7? There's something I need to ask you and I'd rather do it in person. It's not about Maisie. Well. It is a bit.</prop>`,
    `He wrote <b>Yes. 7.</b> and then sat looking at it, because in eight years she had never asked him for anything in person.`,
    `At 10:31 an email arrived that wasn't from counsel.`,
    `<prop type="email" from="Simon Kade, Chairman, Meridian Clearing" to="Henry Ashby" subject="Our minutes" time="10:31 PM">Mr. Ashby, I'm told you've been reading our board minutes. I'm sorry. They're very dull, and that's my fault. I'd like to meet you properly. My assistant will find a time next week. S.K.</prop>`,
    `He read it twice. Then he forwarded it to Vellacott's counsel without replying, and wrote it into the file ${P[9]===3?`the way he'd logged Leonard's call`:`the way he should have logged Leonard's call`}: the time, the sender, what it asked, and that he hadn't answered.`,
    `Under the shopping list, the steps were still on the whiteboard from the morning, with their years: 1909, 1969, 2003, 2026. Every one had been true when somebody set it. He took one of the new green pens out of the box and wrote a fifth year underneath, the one from the top sheet of Dot's folder. Evidence: see 1994. Then he capped the pen and left it there.`,
    `At ten past eleven Maisie rang. It was the first night of spring break, so nobody in Boulder was making her go to bed. ${missed?`She didn't say hello, which since Thursday was how she said hello.`:`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Hi,"</span> she said, as if ringing him were a thing she did.`}`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Mum texted you. It's not bad. I'm not allowed to say."</span> Then, before he could ask anything: <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Can I ask something? What was she like? The diner lady."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Her name was Dot,"</span> he said.`,
    `That was as far as he got. He had the rest of it ready. The green ink, the fork, the sugar packets. And the one that stuck in his throat: every Saturday, before she'd looked at the menu, she'd asked after Maisie.`,
    `None of it would come out. The line was open and quiet, and he could hear Laura's kitchen behind it, the dishwasher running, a cupboard door.`,
    `Maisie waited. Then she said, quite gently for fourteen, <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Okay. You can tell me another time."</span> And after a moment, <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Dot's a good name. It's like a period. Like, that's it, end of sentence."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"She'd have liked that."</span>`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch03.mp3`, len:`about 45 minutes`},
  next:`Third Parties`
});
